this morning a young kentucky man says  his life is on hold because a heart  condition  left him with massive debt despite  having health insurance  we're looking at his case in our  continuing bill of the month partnership  with kai kaiser health news and npr  examining unexpected medical costs the  now 31 year old is dealing with big  bills  and bankruptcy just to be able to live a  normal life after  years of medical care i just stopped  going to the movies i stopped going to  basketball games  matthew fentress was just 25 years old  when he was diagnosed with  cardiomyopathy  a condition that makes it harder for the  heart to pump blood  throughout the body three years ago  doctors inserted a pacemaker and  defibrillator  in fentress to save his life fentress  had  insurance but when he couldn't afford  his five thousand dollar hospital bill  he declared bankruptcy then this january  his cardiologist suggested fentress  undergo a cardiac ablation procedure  to restore normal heart rhythm and offer  him a better quality of life  it's not really living if all i do is  sit around the house  and not be able to enjoy anything i used  to do  the bill for that better life 258  thousand dollars his insurance  negotiated the bill down  and paid more than 26 thousand dollars  but due to his high deductible plan  fentress was on the hook for close to  eight  thousand dollars and you get so many  bills  that it's almost like they pile up on  you  fentress has yet to pay any of the bill  and is filing paperwork to negotiate the  amount with his hospital  but until then he says his life is on  hold can't get a credit card i can't  buy a house i don't see me ever being  out of debt  united healthcare says it's worked with  baptist health louisville  for many years to help reduce health  care costs  it says matthew fentress's procedure was  covered correctly under his plan  which has a maximum out of pocket limit  of seventy nine hundred dollars a year  baptist health louisville says it gave  fentress an estimate of seventy eight  hundred and eighty eight dollars  before the procedure it also says he has  not applied for financial assistance but  is likely eligible  and they're prepared to expedite the  process we're joined now by dr elizabeth  rosenthal editor-in-chief at kaiser  health news  elizabeth good morning thanks for being  with us whenever i watch one of these  stories  i just remember how how you open those  envelopes those bills with such  trepidation  because you don't know what you're going  to see inside but matthew had  insurance so how did he get stuck with  such a large bill  yeah this guy is someone who did  everything right you know you're told to  buy  insurance you don't have a lot of money  so you look at the premium and buy the  cheapest plan or the plan you can afford  the most you can afford  and we're primed to look at the premiums  right  and we're now kind of primed to look at  deductibles as well  but what really hit matthew was not just  the deductibles  but also there's something that's kind  of a nouveau charge called the  co-insurance  so 20 of everything on his hospital bill  he was expected to pay too and that's  what put him over the edge  yeah what is that exactly elizabeth  well it is exactly what it sounds like  everything you're charged for in the  hospital  the patient is expected and some kind of  lower level  cheaper plans to pay 20 percent of and  as we know  you know the the rack rate for that  procedure was  over two hundred thousand dollars  luckily what insurance  does still do is you get the insurance  negotiated rate so it was 2700  but still if you look at all those  different bills it really adds up  and there is an out-of-pocket yearly  maximum in his case it was  i think 8 900 but the thing is  that resets january first so  you know if your procedure and your  complications span two years  you're going to have to pay it twice a  lot of unfairness  yeah and you're lucky if you don't get a  heart attack just from the 250 000  initial bill should people avoid high  deductible plans elizabeth  and if they can't what should they know  about that type of plan  well i you you often can't i mean it's  often what you can afford  so i think we have to be primed as  buyers as smart  shoppers which i hate using that term  for patients but  to look not just at the premiums but at  the  deductibles and at the the co-insurance  payments because that's what really got  this young man into trouble  and ps remember he had something that is  a known  complex he actually got it from the flu  but what he had this myocardial problem  is a problem with kovid too it's it's an  important complication of covid  the heart muscle recovers but the  rhythms are often very abnormal  so he desperately needed this procedure  all right dr elizabeth  rosenthal thank you so much
 

